381 Model Context Protocol (MCP) Authentication
383 The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard for connecting AI agents and LLM tools to data sources and services. You can give your own app an MCP server that runs on Supabase and uses Supabase Auth's OAuth 2.1 capabilities to authenticate AI agents as your existing users. This page covers the authentication side. For the end-to-end walkthrough, including the Edge Function that hosts the server, see Deploy MCP servers.
385 Why use Supabase Auth for MCP?
387 When building MCP servers that connect to your Supabase project, you can use your existing Supabase Auth infrastructure to authenticate AI agents:
389 Use your existing user base - No need to create separate authentication systems; AI agents authenticate as your existing users
391 Standards-compliant OAuth 2.1 - Full implementation with PKCE that MCP clients expect
393 Automatic discovery - MCP clients auto-configure using Supabase's discovery endpoints
395 Dynamic client registration - MCP clients can register themselves automatically with your project
397 Row Level Security - Your existing RLS policies automatically apply to MCP clients
399 User authorization - Users explicitly approve AI agent access through your authorization flow
401 Token management - Automatic refresh token rotation and expiration handled by Supabase
403 How MCP authentication works
405 When you build an MCP server that connects to your Supabase project, authentication flows through Supabase Auth:
407 Discovery: The MCP client fetches your OAuth configuration from Supabase's discovery endpoint
409 Registration (optional): The client registers itself as an OAuth client in your Supabase project
411 Authorization: User is redirected to your authorization endpoint to approve the AI tool's access
413 Token exchange: Supabase issues access and refresh tokens for the authenticated user
415 Authenticated access: The MCP server can now make requests to your Supabase APIs on behalf of the user
417 With Supabase Auth, your MCP server can authenticate AI agents using your existing user accounts without building a separate authentication system.
419 MCP authentication has no separate charge. AI agents authenticate as your existing users, so their sign-ins count toward your project's Monthly Active Users (MAUs). Supabase counts MAUs per distinct user, so multiple agents or MCP clients acting for the same user count as one MAU.
421 Prerequisites
423 Before setting up MCP authentication:
425 Enable OAuth 2.1 server in your Supabase project
427 Build an authorization UI
429 (Optional) Enable dynamic client registration
431 Setting up your MCP server
433 Configure your MCP server to use your Supabase Auth server:
435 1https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1
438 Replace
439 <project-ref> with your project reference ID from the Supabase dashboard.
441 MCP clients will automatically discover your OAuth configuration from:
443 1https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server/auth/v1
446 OAuth client setup
448 Depending on your MCP server implementation, you have two options:
450 Pre-register an OAuth client - Manually register your client by following the Register an OAuth client guide and use the client credentials in your MCP server
452 Dynamic client registration - Enable this in Authentication > OAuth Server in your Supabase dashboard to allow MCP clients to register themselves automatically without manual intervention
454 Dynamic registration allows any MCP client to register with your project. Consider:
456 Requiring user approval for all clients
458 Monitoring registered clients regularly
460 Validating redirect URIs are from trusted domains
