196 How to fix: Register the URL your server actually listens on, not a URL that redirects to it. Common culprits are apex-to-
197 www. canonicalization, geographic or region routing, and vanity-domain-to-CDN redirects.
199 OAuth discovery fails
201 If your server requires authentication, Claude performs OAuth discovery before it can connect. A discovery failure can surface as “Couldn’t reach” even though your MCP endpoint itself is reachable, or as a sign-in that redirects to
202 /authorize on your MCP server’s host and fails there. The redirect happens when Claude can’t read your RFC 9728 protected resource metadata and falls back to treating your MCP server’s origin as the authorization server, so the browser opens a sign-in page that doesn’t exist on your server. These are the most common causes:
204 Discovery metadata returns 404: if your
205 401 response doesn’t include a
206 WWW-Authenticate header with a
207 resource_metadata pointer, Claude looks for protected resource metadata and authorization server metadata at the standard
208 /.well-known/ paths on your MCP server’s origin. If those paths return
209 404 and you haven’t pointed Claude elsewhere, Claude can’t locate your authorization server
211 No way to register a client: Claude needs one of RFC 7591 dynamic client registration (a
212 registration_endpoint in your authorization server metadata), Client ID Metadata Documents (
213 "client_id_metadata_document_supported": true), or a pre-registered client. Without any of these, Claude can’t obtain a client identity. See supported authentication types
215 Authorization server is on a different host than the MCP server: Claude discovers protected resource metadata from your MCP server, then makes a second round of discovery requests against the authorization server host listed in
216 authorization_servers. If that host is behind a different CDN or WAF, it must also be reachable from Anthropic’s egress range. See Serve discovery metadata
218 A proxy or hosting platform alters the discovery response: a layer in front of your server can rename or drop the
219 WWW-Authenticate header, or answer
220 403 on the
221 /.well-known/ paths before the request reaches your application. Check the response at the deployed edge with
222 curl from a public network, not with a tool that runs inside your platform
224 How to check: From a public network, request each discovery document:
226 curl -i https://your-server.example.com/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource
227 curl -i https://your-server.example.com/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
228 curl -i https://your-server.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration
230 If your MCP endpoint includes a path component, such as
231 https://your-server.example.com/mcp, append it to the well-known path:
232 /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp.
233 The protected resource metadata should return
234 200 with valid JSON. For authorization server metadata, your server only needs to answer one of the two discovery endpoints. Claude tries
235 /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server (RFC 8414) first, then falls back to
236 /.well-known/openid-configuration (OpenID Connect Discovery 1.0). A
237 404 on one is expected if the other returns
238 200. Most hosted identity providers, including Auth0, Okta, Microsoft Entra, Keycloak, and Supabase Auth, only serve
239 /.well-known/openid-configuration.
240 Whichever metadata document resolves should advertise a
241 registration_endpoint (DCR),
242 "client_id_metadata_document_supported": true (CIMD), or you should be using pre-registered credentials. In a cross-host setup, run the protected-resource curl against your MCP server and the two authorization-server curls against your authorization server’s issuer host.
244 Authorization with the MCP server failed
246 This error usually appears after the OAuth flow has started. These are the most common causes:
248 Issuer mismatch: the
249 issuer value in your authorization server metadata must match the issuer that signs your tokens. If your tokens come from a third-party identity provider but your metadata advertises a different issuer URL, validation can fail
251 Audience mismatch: the MCP authorization spec requires your server to verify each access token was issued for it. Claude sends the RFC 8707
252 resource parameter on authorization and token requests, set to the canonical form of your MCP server URL, including any path component. The canonical form has a lowercase scheme and host, no trailing slash, no fragment, and no default port. Your authorization server should issue tokens with that audience, and your MCP server should accept the canonical value when checking
253 aud rather than doing a strict byte-for-byte comparison against what the user typed. Or use whatever audience-binding mechanism your token format supports, as long as it confirms the token was minted for your server and not another service
255 PKCE not supported: Claude includes a PKCE
256 code_challenge with
257 code_challenge_method=S256 in every authorization request. If your authorization server doesn’t implement S256 PKCE, the flow fails at the token endpoint. The MCP authorization spec also requires authorization servers to advertise
258 "code_challenge_methods_supported": ["S256"] so spec-compliant clients can verify support before starting the flow
260 Refresh failures: use RFC 6749-compliant error codes when a refresh token expires. See token refresh
