--- max2697 2026-10-03T00:22:33Z 
 I’m seeing this too with a hosted Supabase OAuth 2.1 project. My MCP client included resource in the authorization request, but the issued token had aud: "authenticated". A refresh request explicitly including the same resource produced the same audience, so my MCP server rejected both tokens.
Could you prioritize RFC 8707 resource-bound tokens through authorization, exchange, and refresh, or share the expected path to support them? I’d be happy to test a preview. Thanks!
--- cadeskriacao 2026-10-06T03:18:53Z 
 Confirming on a hosted project (October 2026), and adding the security side of this. It goes beyond spec compliance.

- `/oauth/authorize` accepts any absolute URI as `resource`. `resource=https://evil.example/mcp` goes straight to the consent page, and only a non-URI is refused ("resource must be an absolute URI").
- A token issued with `resource=https://evil.example/mcp` on both authorize and token carries `aud: "authenticated"`. No claim records the resource.

**Why it is a security issue, not only a compliance one.** With dynamic registration on, a user can add a malicious MCP server whose protected-resource metadata names our project as its authorization server. Their own client (Claude or ChatGPT, with a legitimate registration and callback) gets a token "for" that server. Every token has the same audience, so that server can replay it against our MCP server and against PostgREST. The resource server cannot refuse it, and neither can the consent page: `getAuthorizationDetails` does not expose `resource` either.

**A smaller step that would already unblock projects:** pass `resource` to the Custom Access Token hook, and expose it in `getAuthorizationDetails`. Projects could then set `aud`, or warn on the consent screen, while full RFC 8707 support lands.
