464 Expose local instance to the world
466 To test OAuth flows with external applications, you can expose your local Supabase instance using a tunnel solution (such as ngrok or Cloudflare Tunnel).
468 When using a tunnel, set
469 auth.jwt_issuer in your config file to match your tunnel URL:
470 supabase/config.tomlsupabase/config.jsonc
472 1[auth]2jwt_issuer = "https://my-tunnel.url/auth/v1"
475 1{2 "auth": {3 "jwt_issuer": "https://my-tunnel.url/auth/v1"4 }5}
477 This ensures that JWTs issued by your local instance use the correct issuer claim for token validation, and serves the discovery endpoint at the correct location with accurate discovery information.
479 Use asymmetric JWT signing keys for better security
481 By default, Supabase uses HS256 (symmetric) for signing JWTs. For OAuth use cases, we recommend migrating to asymmetric algorithms like RS256 or ES256. Asymmetric keys are more scalable and secure because:
483 OAuth clients can validate JWTs using the public key from your JWKS endpoint
485 No need to share your JWT secret with third-party applications
487 More resilient architecture for distributed systems
489 Learn more about configuring JWT signing keys.
491 Note: If you plan to use OpenID Connect ID tokens (by requesting the
492 openid scope), asymmetric signing algorithms are required. ID token generation will fail with HS256.
494 Configure your authorization path
496 Before registering clients, you need to configure where your authorization UI will live.
498 In your project dashboard, navigate to Authentication > OAuth Server
500 Set the Authorization Path (e.g.,
501 /oauth/consent)
503 The authorization path is combined with your Site URL (configured in Authentication > URL Configuration) to create the full authorization endpoint URL.
505 Your authorization UI will be at the combined Site URL + Authorization Path. For example:
507 Site URL:
508 https://example.com (from Authentication > URL Configuration)
510 Authorization Path:
511 /oauth/consent (from OAuth Server settings)
513 Your authorization UI:
514 https://example.com/oauth/consent
516 When OAuth clients initiate the authorization flow, Supabase Auth will redirect users to this URL with an
517 authorization_id query parameter. You'll use Supabase JavaScript library OAuth methods to handle the authorization:
519 supabase.auth.oauth.getAuthorizationDetails(authorization_id) - Retrieve client and authorization details
521 supabase.auth.oauth.approveAuthorization(authorization_id) - Approve the authorization request
523 supabase.auth.oauth.denyAuthorization(authorization_id) - Deny the authorization request
525 Build your authorization UI
527 This is where you build the frontend for your authorization flow. When third-party apps initiate OAuth, users will be redirected to your authorization path (configured in the previous step) with an
528 authorization_id query parameter.
530 Your authorization UI should:
532 Extract authorization_id - Get the
533 authorization_id from the URL query parameters
535 Authenticate the user - If not already signed in, redirect to your sign-in page (preserving the authorization_id)
537 Retrieve authorization details - Use
538 supabase.auth.oauth.getAuthorizationDetails(authorization_id) to get client information including requested scopes
540 Display consent screen - Show the user what app is requesting access and what scopes/permissions are being requested (available as a pre-built web component)
542 Handle user decision - Call either
543 approveAuthorization(authorization_id) or
544 denyAuthorization(authorization_id) based on user choice
546 The authorization details include a
547 scope field (singular) containing a space-separated string of scopes requested by the client (e.g.,
548 "openid email profile"). You should display these scopes to the user so they understand what information will be shared.
550 This is a frontend implementation. You're building the UI that displays the consent screen and handles user interactions. The actual OAuth token generation is handled by Supabase Auth after you call the approve/deny methods.
