=== media-ctrl 2026-11-14T17:04:41Z association: NONE
Your log stops after step 3 and no registration or authorize request follows. That is the signature of a client that fetched metadata and then rejected it, so the tree to look at is metadata validation, not DCR and not your server.

The path is the likely culprit. Your resource metadata lists the authorization server as:

    https://<project>.supabase.co/auth/v1

That issuer has a path component. RFC 8414 section 3.1 is explicit about where metadata lives in that case: any terminating slash is removed, then /.well-known/ and the well-known suffix are inserted between the host and the path. So the metadata document for this issuer lives at:

    https://<project>.supabase.co/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server/auth/v1

not at the host root. Two distinct things can go wrong from there, and they are worth separating before anything else:

1. That path-inserted URL returns 404. A client that requires metadata to be derived from the issuer aborts exactly where your log stops: no register, no authorize, no callback.
2. That URL returns a document whose "issuer" value is the root, https://<project>.supabase.co, while your resource metadata points at the /auth/v1 issuer. The two disagree and a client that compares them rejects the authorization server. Fetching the root /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server, which is what step 3 in your log shows, describes the root issuer and not the one you need.

Can you curl the path-inserted URL and paste the issuer value from both documents? If they differ, that is the whole bug and it is on the metadata layout rather than on Claude's side. Supabase serves the auth endpoints under /auth/v1, so this is a plausible mismatch between where the document is published and which issuer it declares.

Two smaller things that also abort clients at this stage, from RFC 8414 section 2: response_types_supported and authorization_endpoint are REQUIRED in the document, and token_endpoint is required unless only implicit is supported. Your summary names code_challenge_methods_supported and client_secret_post but not those. A full paste of the metadata JSON would settle it faster than any further retries.

The other detail worth keeping: you got the same failure with a pre-registered confidential client as with DCR. That rules out registration and points the same way, at metadata validation happening before client resolution.

We hit this class while wiring an OAuth proxy in front of a multi-vendor MCP server, where the proxy published metadata at the host root while the issuer carried a path. Clients that derive the well-known URL from the issuer never found the document and failed with a generic "couldn't be added" and an empty server log, which is what sent us looking at the wrong layer for a day.
